    31	## 3. Lemma Z: the inverse zero test is optimality verification
    32	
    33	For any listed distance, the exact optimum is zero if and only if x0 minimizes c·x over F. If x0 is optimal, d=c is feasible and has distance zero. Conversely, distance zero implies d=c since every weight is positive; inverse feasibility then says that x0 is optimal under c. This also works if the inverse algorithm returns only its exact minimizing d: test d=c. If the inverse problem imposes additional allowed-cost constraints, this lemma requires c itself to be allowed. It does not allow queries outside the advertised cost domain.
    34	
    35	An approximation oracle is not substituted for this exact zero test. For general norms, one cannot decide whether an approximate numerical zero is mathematically zero without further separation bounds.
