   136	Then T belongs to UP, the class of languages recognized by polynomial-time nondeterministic machines with at most one accepting computation per input. Consequently, if T is NP-hard under polynomial many-one reductions, NP=UP. This is a conditional barrier, not a proof that P=NP or that NP!=UP. No literature-novelty claim is made for this elementary consequence.
