   171	## 8d. Independent binary features are another solved boundary
   172	
   173	Suppose f(c,x)=sum_{j=1}^m c_j phi_j(x), where m is polynomial, every phi_j(x) is binary and polynomial-time computable, all c_j are independently mutable, and phi contains the original coordinates x_1,...,x_n. Then the image F'={phi(x):x in F} is a binary feasible family with polynomial-time membership: read its first n coordinates as x, check x in F, and recompute phi(x). A supplied x0 yields phi(x0), and changing feature costs is exactly the advertised original parameter change. Apply Theorem F to F' and recover x from those first n coordinates. Therefore inverse in P implies forward in P here as well. This covers fully independently parametrized pseudo-Boolean polynomial objectives whose feature lists contain all linear monomials, including full quadratic objectives with linear terms. It does not cover arbitrary nonlinear dependence on costs or tied coefficients.
