   175	## 8e. Precisely retained general question and failed attack
   176	
   177	The remaining research question is the converse for **general objectives**, not an invented claim that the linear or bottleneck cases are still open. One finite encoded refinement of Heuberger's equations is:
   178	
   179	> Fix a uniform family of polynomial-time computable rational-valued objectives f_n(c,x), with polynomial output bit length, f_n(0,x)=0, all rational parameters c in Q^{p(n)}, binary feasible sets F(I) with polynomial membership and a supplied feasible start, and global rather than local optimality. Suppose the unconstrained weighted-l1 inverse problem, given any c and any x0 in F(I), always has a rational minimizing parameter of polynomial encoding length and is exactly polynomial-time solvable. Must forward global optimization be polynomial-time solvable?
   180	
   181	The attainment/encoding premise is explicit because a general nonlinear inverse constraint need not have a rational or even attained optimum. The normalization f_n(0,x)=0 prevents making the inverse artificially infeasible at inconvenient targets. The supplied start removes feasibility-only hardness. The zero-test lemma still applies, but our linear augmentation proof requires constructing parameter changes that penalize disagreement with a target without otherwise changing comparisons; an arbitrary f_n need not permit those changes. Bit scaling also need not preserve the objective structure. The bottleneck threshold argument relies on a special subset-existence predicate unavailable for arbitrary f_n. Feature lifting works only when the independent-coordinate and recoverability hypotheses of Section 8d hold.
   182	
   183	We have not proved or refuted this general encoded implication. Heuberger's general equations and the 2025 broad-open wording motivate it, but **no opened source states this exact strengthened encoding/normalization refinement verbatim**. Thus its designation here is OPEN as an unresolved research target, not a claim that its precise formulation has a certified community-wide open-status theorem. The book's full general question remains unsolved in this dossier; neither Proposition C nor the rediscovery of Schulz is substituted for it.
   184	
   185	## 9. What is and is not settled
   186	
   187	1. Full-linear binary problems with arbitrary signed costs and a known/easily found feasible start: **settled affirmatively in prior literature (Schulz 2009)**. The preceding proof is a reproduced argument with executable exact checks. Hamming inverse-P implies forward-P follows by the same zero-test implication; no equivalence for sum-Hamming is asserted.
   188	2. Broad models allowing restricted costs and feasibility-bearing forward tasks: **the literal universal implication is false under P!=NP**, by C. The construction is intentionally degenerate and is not advertised as solving the intended nondegenerate question.
   189	3. Full-cost binary families with arbitrary implicit feasibility, no supplied/easily found initial point, and no dummy closure: **not resolved here**. Our algorithms need their first feasible target. The restricted-cost examples do not provide a counterexample to this exact remaining statement. Proposition U shows that an NP-hard threshold counterexample would imply NP=UP; Proposition O rules out a completely generic membership/inverse-oracle shortcut, but is not an explicit-input counterexample. We make no claim that the literature has proved this most general remaining formulation open; our search did not settle it.
   190	4. The known-start minmax/bottleneck converse and independently parametrized recoverable binary-feature objectives also hold, by Sections 8c–8d. The normalized general-objective refinement in Section 8e remains **OPEN here**. Changing constraints/edge lengths, arbitrary real feasible sets, partial target observations, and KKT points in place of global optima require separate theorems. The July 2025 Springer chapter still calls its broad NP-hard-forward/P-inverse question unresolved in its public abstract. This is evidence about that publication, not a contradiction to Schulz's explicitly conditional theorem or proof that every specialized refinement is open.
