   376	(QP ) if and only if Q̄ 6= Q or c̄ 6= c. Since (QP ) have many feasible solutions (more than exponential
   377	number), so P 2 is in NP.
   378	    Using theorem 4.3 and theorem 4.4 we have the following main result of the section.
   379	
   380	Theorem 4.5      CoNP=NP.
   381	
   382	                                           5. Conclusions
   383	
   384	
   385	    In this paper we have shown that the inverse problem of determining the KKT point of the
   386	non-convex quadratic programming is polynomial solvable. We also showed that the inverse prob-
   387	lems of determining the optimal solutions of the non-monotone linear complementarity problem in
   388	cases (a) and (a) are polynomial, in case (c) is NP-hard. We showed that the inverse problem
   389	of determining a KKT point of the non-convex (QP ) with l2 norm is equivalent to solve a convex
   390	quadratic programming, therefore can be solved by polynomial interior point algorithms. We then
   391	showed that the inverse problems of determining an optimal solution of the non-monotone linear
   392	complementarity problem with l2 norm in cases (a) and (b) are also equivalent to solve a convex
   393	quadratic programming problem, hence can also be solved by polynomial interior point algorithms.
   394	In case (c), we showed that the inverse problem of non-monotone LCP with l1 norm is equivalent to
   395	solve an optimization problem with linear objective function and n non-convex quadratic constraints,
   396	therefore is NP-hard. The results in this paper solved an open question raised by Heuberger in [7]
   397	on whether there exists an NP-hard problem such that its inverse problem is polynomial solvable. If
   398	so one proves CoNP=NP, a long standing open problem in the theory of computational complexity.
   399	    It is also interesting to observe that finding the KKT points of a general quadratic programming
   400	problem and solving the non-convex linear complementary problem are the only NP-complete opti-
   401	mization problems we have found so far whose inverse problems are polynomial solvable. Are there
   402	any other NP-complete optimization problems whose inverse problems are polynomial solvable? The
   403	main difficulty is that there are no optimality conditions for most of the NP-complete optimization
   404	problems.
   405	
