    66	An optimization problem is that of determining a member of a feasible set (an optimal solution) that mini-
    67	mizes the value of a given objective function. The feasible set is typically described as the points in a vector
    68	space satisfying a given set of equations, inequalities, and disjunctions (usually in the form of a requirement
    69	that the value of a certain element of the solution take on values in a discrete set).
    70	An inverse optimization problem, in contrast, is a related problem in which the description of the original
    71	forward optimization problem is incomplete (some parameters are missing or cannot be observed), but a full
    72	or partial solution can be observed. The goal is to determine values for the missing parameters with respect
    73	to which the given solution would be optimal for the resulting complete problem. Estimates for the missing
    74	parameters may be given, in which case the goal is to produce a set of parameters that is as close to the
    75	given estimates as possible by some metric.
    76	The forward optimization problem of interest in this paper is the mixed integer linear optimization problem
    77	
    78	                                                   max d> x,                                           (MILP)
    79	                                                   x∈S
    80	
    81	where d ∈ Qn and
    82	                                    S = {x ∈ Rn | Ax ≤ b} ∩ (Zr × Rn−r )
    83	for A ∈ Qm×n , b ∈ Qm and for some nonnegative integer r. In the case when r = 0, (MILP) is known simply
    84	as a linear optimization problem (LP).
    85	One can associate a number of different inverse problems with (MILP), depending on what parts of the
    86	description (A, b, d) are unknown and what form the objective function of the inverse problem takes. Here,
    87	we study the case in which the objective function d of the forward problem is the unknown element of the
    88	input, but in which A and b, along with a target vector x0 ∈ Qn , are given. A feasible solution to the inverse
    89	problem (which we refer to as a feasible objective) is any dˆ ∈ Rn for which dˆ> x0 = maxx∈S dˆ> x.
    90	It is important in the analysis that follows to be precise about the assumptions on the target vector x0 .
    91	Our initial informal statement of the problem implicitly assumed that x0 ∈ S, since otherwise, x0 cannot
    92	technically be an optimal solution, regardless of the objective function chosen. On the other hand, neither the
    93	more precise mathematical definition given in the preceding paragraph nor the mathematical formulations we
    94	introduce shortly require x0 ∈ S and both can be interpreted even when x0 6∈ S. As a practical matter when
    95	solving inverse problems in practice, this subtle distinction is usually not very important, since membership
    96	in S can be verified in a preprocessing step if necessary. However, in the context of complexity analysis and
    97	in considering the relationship of the inverse problem to the related separation and optimization problems,
    98	this point is important and we return to it. For example, if we do not make this assumption, the inverse
    99	optimization problem can be seen to be equivalent to both the separation problem and the problem of
   100	verifying a given dual bound on the optimal solution value of an MILP.
   101	For these and other reasons that will become clear, we do not assume x0 ∈ S, but this may make some
   102	aspects of what follows a bit ambiguous. To resolve any ambiguity, we replace S with the augmented set
   103	S + = S ∪ {x0 }, when appropriate, in the remainder of the paper.
