   106	1.1    Formulations
   107	
   108	We now present several mathematical formulations of what we refer to from now on as the inverse mixed
   109	integer linear optimization problem (IMILP). A straightforward formulation of this problem that explains
   110	why we refer to the general class of problems as “inverse” problems is as that of computing the mathematical
   111	inverse of a function that is parametric in some part of the input to a given problem instance. In this case,
   112	the relevant function is
   113	                                           φ(d) = argmaxx∈S + d> x.
   114	
   115	
   116	
   117	                                                         3
   118	In terms of the function φ, a feasible objective is any element of the preimage φ−1 (x0 ). To make the IMILP
   119	an optimization problem in itself, we add an objective function f : Qn → Q, to obtain the general formulation
   120	
   121	                                                    min        f (d).                                      (INV)
   122	                                                 d∈φ−1 (x0 )
