   844	4     Complexity of Inverse MILP
   845	
   846	In this section, we apply the framework discussed in Section 3 to analyze the complexity of the inverse MILP.
   847	We follow the traditional approach and describe the complexity of the decision versions. In addition to the
   848	standard primal bound verification problem, we also consider the dual bound and optimal value verification
   849	problems. We show that the primal bound, dual bound, and optimal value verification problems for the
   850	inverse MILP are in the complexity classes coNP-complete, NP-complete, and DP -complete, respectively.
   851	Hence, the classes associated with primal and dual bounding problems are reversed when going from MILP
   852	to inverse MILP, while the optimal value verification problems are both contained in the same class.
   853	
   854	
   855	4.1    Polynomially Solvable Cases
   856	
   857	The result of Ahuja and Orlin [2001] can be applied directly to observe that there are cases of the inverse
   858	MILP that are polynomially solvable. In particular, they showed that the inverse problem can be solved in
   859	polynomial time whenever the forward problem is polynomially solvable.
   860	Theorem 2. (Ahuja and Orlin [2001]) If an optimization problem is polynomially solvable for each linear
   861	cost function, then the corresponding inverse problems under the `1 and `∞ norms are polynomially solvable.
   862	
   863	Ahuja and Orlin [2001] use Theorem 1 from Grötschel et al. [1993] to conclude that inverse LP, in particular,
   864	is polynomially solvable. The separation problem in this case is an LP of polynomial input size and is hence
   865	polynomially solvable. The theorem of Grötschel et al. [1993] shows that both problems are in the class P,
   866	since Karp and Cook reductions are equivalent for problems in P. Theorem 2 also indicates that if a given
   867	MILP is polynomially solvable, then the associated inverse problem is also polynomially solvable.
   868	
   869	
   870	4.2    General Case
   871	
   872	In the general case, the MILP constituting the forward problem is not known to be polynomially solv-
   873	able and so we now consider MILPs whose decision versions are complete for NP. Applying the re-
   874	sults of Grötschel et al. [1993] straightforwardly, as Ahuja and Orlin [2001] did, we can easily show
   875	that (IMILP-L1) and (IMILP-INF) can be solved in polynomial time, given an oracle for the MPVP, as
   876	stated in the following theorem.
   877	Theorem 3. Given an oracle for the MPVP, (IMILP-L1) and (IMILP-INF) are solvable in time polynomial
   878	in n, the vertex complexity of conv(S + ), and hci.
   879	
   880	The above result directly implies that IMILP under `1 and `∞ norms is in fact in the complexity ∆P2 , but
   881	stronger results are possible, as we show. In the remainder of this section, we assume the norm used is a
   882	p-norm, as this is needed for some results (in particular, Proposition 1 is crucially used).
   883	
   884	
