   918	Informal Discussion. Before presenting the formal proofs, which are somewhat technical, we informally
   919	describe the relationship of the inverse optimization problem to the MILP analogues of the above decision
   920	problems, which are described in Section 3.2. Suppose we are given a value α and we wish to determine
   921	whether it is a primal bound, a dual bound, or the exact optimal value of (MILP) with objective function
   922	vector c ∈ Qn . Roughly speaking, we can utilize an algorithm for solving (IMILP) to make the determination,
   923	as follows. We first construct the target vector
   924	                                                             c
   925	                                                xα := α          ,
   926	                                                           kck22
   927	
   928	which has an objective function value (in the forward problem) of c> xα = α by construction. Now suppose
   929	we solve (IMILP) with xα as the target vector and c as the estimated objective function coefficient. Note
   930	that xα is not necessarily in S. Solving this inverse problem will yield one of two results.
   931	
   932	  1. The optimal value of the inverse problem is 0. This means that xα ∈ argmaxx∈S + c> x, which imme-
   933	     diately implies that α ≥ maxx∈S c> x and we have that α is a valid dual bound for (MILP).
   934	  2. The optimal value of the inverse problem is strictly positive. Then there must be a point x ∈ S
   935	     for which c> x > c> xα = α, which means that α < maxx∈S c> x and α is a (strict) primal bound
   936	     for (MILP).
   937	
   938	There are a number of challenges to be overcome in moving from this informal argument to the formal
   939	reductions in the completeness proofs. One obstacle is that the above argument does not precisely establish
   940	the status of α as a bound because we cannot distinguish between when α is a strict dual bound (and hence
   941	not a primal bound) and when α is the exact optimal value. This can be overcome essentially by appealing
   942	to Proposition 1 to reformulate certain strict inequalities as standard inequalities (see Lemma 3). A second
   943	challenge is that we have only described a reduction of a decision version of MILP to an optimization
   944	
