    93	2 Preliminaries
    94	This chapter is concerned with linear combinatorial optimization problems. A
    95	linear combinatorial optimization problem Π consists of a family of instances
    96	(N, F, c), where N = {1, 2, . . . , n} is the ground set, F ⊆ P  2N is the set of
    97	                                            n
    98	feasible solutions, and the vector c ∈ Z assigns a cost of i∈F ci to every
    99	feasible solution F ∈ F.1 The objective is to find a feasible solution of minimal
   100	cost.2 The set of feasible solutions of a combinatorial optimization problem is
   101	usually not described explicitly, but is given implicitly, such as the family of
   102	all stable sets in an undirected graph, or the set of all Hamiltonian tours in
   103	a directed graph. Typically, if we have one instance of such a combinatorial
   104	optimization problem, then the same instance in which only the objective
   105	function coefficients are changed, is also an instance of the same combinatorial
   106	optimization problem. We make this our first assumption.
   107	Assumption 1. Let Π be a linear combinatorial optimization problem. If
   108	(N, F, c) describes an instance of Π, and d ∈ Zn , then (N, F, d) is an in-
   109	stance of Π as well.
   110	    Our interpretation of this assumption is that certain computational prop-
   111	erties of the problems considered here, such as polynomial-time solvability,
   112	depend only on the structure of F, but not on that of c.3 As a consequence of
   113	Assumption 1, we henceforth use Π to just denote the family of pairs (N, F),
   114	knowing that each such pair together with any compatible objective function
   115	vector constitutes an instance of the associated optimization problem. This
   116	convention allows us to refer to Π, even if we consider algorithmic problems
   117	other than optimization, which are defined over the instances (N, F) of Π.
   118	    It is well known that the optimization problem associated with Π can be
   119	stated equivalently as a 0/1-integer programming problem. Namely,
