   974	4    Conclusions
   975	In this paper, we considered general minimum-cost inverse optimization problems in the con-
   976	strained setting, i.e. with lower and upper bounds on the coordinates of the deviation vector.
   977	We provided simple, purely combinatorial algorithms for the weighted bottleneck Hamming
   978	distance and ℓ∞ -norm objectives. The algorithms follow a scheme that resembles Newton’s
   979	algorithm, and find an optimal deviation vector in strongly polynomial when the bottleneck
   980	Hamming distance, and in pseudo-polynomial time when the ℓ∞ -norm is considered. For both
   981	objectives, we extended the results extend to inverse optimization problems with multiple cost
   982	functions.
   983	    Despite the extensive literature on inverse optimization, only few results are known when the
   984	desired deviation vector p is required to be integral, see e.g. [1, 11, 12]. If ℓ, u and c are integral
   985	vectors, then the deviation vector p[δ|ℓ,u|w] defined in the bottleneck Hamming distance case
   986	is integral independently from the choice of δ, therefore the fractional and integral optimums
   987	coincide. For the unweighted ℓ∞ -norm objective, i.e. when in addition w ≡ 1 holds, the deviation
   988	vector p[δ|ℓ,u|w] might not be integral as we assign value δ to some of its coordinates. However,
   989	Lemmas 6 and 7 together imply that if p[δ|ℓ,u|w] is an optimal fractional deviation vector, then
   990	p[⌈δ⌉|ℓ,u|w] is an optimal integral deviation vector.
   991	    Though the proposed algorithm is capable of solving minimum cost inverse optimization
   992	problems in a very general setting, it naturally has its limitations. In particular, it is not suitable
   993	for handling deeper connections between the coordinates of the cost function. For example, if
   994	the underlying optimization problem is a minimum cost s-t path problem in a directed graph
   995	with conservative arc-costs c, then it is not clear how to implement the algorithm as to maintain
   996	conservativeness throughout.
   997	    In an accompanying paper [5], we consider minimum-cost inverse optimization problems
   998	under the weighted span objective, and provide a min-max characterization for the weighted span
   999	of an optimal deviation vector in the unconstrained setting, as well as an efficient algorithm for
  1000	the constrained setting.

