   428	This linear program can be solved using the revised simplex method and starting with y 1 = y 2 = 0.
   429	Pivot columns can be chosen as follows: Either we take one of the 4n columns that correspond to
   430	y 1 , y 2 , and the slack variables, or we take the column that corresponds to some y gx . The latter is
   431	the case if and only if the constraint (c + α − β)t x ≥ (c + α − β)t x̂g in (3.11) is violated. In order
   432	to find such a constraint, we have to solve the original optimization problem (2.1) for all g ∈ G.
   433	Therefore, in each step, we have to calculate the current dual solution (α, β) and we have to check
   434	whether for all g ∈ G the solution x̂ is optimum under costs ĉ = c + α − β. If this is true, we stop,
   435	if not, a pivot column has been found.
   436	      To summarize, this method has the advantage that no linear programming formulation of the
   437	original problem has to be used. The price is that the original problem has to be solved many
   438	times. Numerical experiments have been carried out for the inverse shortest paths problem by
   439	Zhang, Ma, and Yang [43] and for several inverse problems by Yang [29].
