sources/literature-notes.md:5:## Load-bearing sources actually opened
sources/literature-notes.md:9:2. **Heuberger (2004)**, DOI [10.1023/B:JOCO.0000038914.26975.9b](https://doi.org/10.1023/B%3AJOCO.0000038914.26975.9b). Full author manuscript opened and saved as `heuberger-2004.pdf` / `.txt`; publisher record also opened. This manuscript is 27 PDF pages, not the 33-page typeset journal layout. Section 2.1, equations (2.1)–(2.2), permits F_g subsets of R^n, a fixed general f(c,x), supplied feasible targets, and lower/upper bounds on modified costs. The objective need not be c·x. Section 5, item 1, author-PDF page 23, asks (verbatim, line breaks normalized):
sources/literature-notes.md:15:3. **Schulz (2009)**, DOI [10.1007/978-3-540-76796-1_19](https://doi.org/10.1007/978-3-540-76796-1_19), chapter pp.399–428. Full MIT author manuscript and publisher record opened. Saved `schulz-2009.pdf` / `.txt`, publisher metadata `schulz-metadata.json`.
sources/literature-notes.md:21:   This source decisively resolves the standard known-start linear binary reading, even though later broad surveys still use open-problem wording.
sources/literature-notes.md:23:4. **Huang (2005)**, DOI [10.1007/11496199_45](https://doi.org/10.1007/11496199_45). Opened publisher abstract, saved HTML `huang-2005.html`. It explicitly says the inverse knapsack algorithm and fixed-number-of-constraints integer-programming inverse algorithm are **pseudo-polynomial**. Full paid chapter was not obtained. Therefore this is not evidence of bit-polynomial inverse algorithms for NP-hard forward problems. Bulut–Ralphs independently describes the same result in its previous-work section.
sources/literature-notes.md:27:6. **Bulut–Ralphs (2021)**, DOI [10.1137/20M1377369](https://doi.org/10.1137/20M1377369), arXiv [2104.09002](https://arxiv.org/abs/2104.09002). Full author report, last revised November 18, 2021, opened and saved as `bulut-ralphs-2021.pdf` / `.txt`. It proves coNP-completeness of inverse-MILP primal-bound verification, NP-completeness of dual-bound verification, and DP-completeness of exact-optimal-value verification. **Important model caveat:** Section 1 expressly does not require x0 in S; it works with S+=S union {x0}. Its general equivalence therefore cannot be transferred blindly to a promised-feasible-target oracle. The dummy-closure theorem in our proof makes a corresponding assumption explicit. Relevant local anchors: `.txt:844-914` (decision formulations), `:918-936` (target may be infeasible in S).
sources/literature-notes.md:29:7. **Guan–Pardalos–Zhang (2025)**, DOI [10.1007/978-3-031-91175-0_1](https://doi.org/10.1007/978-3-031-91175-0_1), first online July 25, 2025. Publisher abstract and references opened; saved `guan-pardalos-zhang-2025.html`. The abstract explicitly says: “We question whether an inverse problem of an NP-hard problem can be polynomially solvable, an unresolved challenge.” **Only the public abstract/references were available**, not the chapter's full formulation/discussion. This supports reporting its broad question as described by that publication, not asserting that every restricted version remained open in 2025. It cites Bulut–Ralphs. Its publicly shown reference list did not supply the missing distinction as a replacement for reading Schulz's actual theorem.
sources/literature-notes.md:31:8. **Bérczi–Mendoza-Cadena–Varga (2025 journal / 2023 preprint)**, DOI [10.1007/s11590-024-02183-0](https://doi.org/10.1007/s11590-024-02183-0), arXiv [2302.13411](https://arxiv.org/abs/2302.13411). Full arXiv PDF and abstract opened, saved `berczi-mendoza-varga.pdf` / `.txt`; final publisher article also opened and saved as `berczi-mendoza-varga-journal-2025.pdf` / `.txt`.
sources/literature-notes.md:37:9. **Ley–Merkert (2025)**, DOI [10.1007/s10898-025-01529-x](https://doi.org/10.1007/s10898-025-01529-x). Full open-access article opened and PDF saved `ley-merkert-2025.pdf` / `.txt`. With only weight increases, partial inverse shortest path on a DAG and partial inverse assignment are NP-complete even with a single required edge/arc. This concerns *partial* targets and the opposite forward-P/inverse-hard direction; it is not a counterexample to inverse-P => forward-P.
sources/literature-notes.md:39:10. **Cai–Yang–Zhang (1999)**, DOI [10.1023/A:1008360312607](https://doi.org/10.1023/A%3A1008360312607). Publisher abstract opened and saved `cai-yang-zhang-1999.html`: forward center location is polynomial, inverse is NP-hard. Heuberger Section 4.18 additionally specifies sign-constrained single-object unit-l1 inverse minmax center location, all vertex demands 1. The book's stronger adjective “strongly” is **[UNVERIFIED] from the primary full proof** because the original article was not obtained. We report only the primary abstract's NP-hardness, which suffices here.
sources/literature-notes.md:43:**Demange–Monnot**, *An Introduction to Inverse Combinatorial Problems*, DOI [10.1002/9781119005353.ch17](https://doi.org/10.1002/9781119005353.ch17), July 10, 2014, pp.547–586. The publisher URL and both 2014/earlier-edition PDF endpoints returned HTTP 403. Publisher-deposited Crossref metadata was opened and saved in `demange-monnot-metadata.json`, confirming title/authors/date/DOI but providing no abstract. Search located no accessible author manuscript. **[UNVERIFIED]** whether this chapter resolves or qualifies the converse. No theorem or open-status claim is attributed to its unseen contents.
sources/literature-notes.md:45:**Ahuja–Orlin (2001)**, DOI [10.1287/opre.49.5.771.10607](https://doi.org/10.1287/opre.49.5.771.10607). The publisher returned 403, the indexed CiteSeer mirror 404; Orlin's author publication list confirms the correct journal metadata. MIT DSpace record [1721.1/2696](https://hdl.handle.net/1721.1/2696) for the 1998 working paper opened, but its PDF download returned 429. We rely on the fully opened Heuberger Theorem 3.3 and Schulz Lemma 4 for the theorem itself, not an invented reading of the inaccessible journal PDF.
sources/literature-notes.md:49:**Siming Huang (2021 preprint)**, [arXiv:2103.15337](https://arxiv.org/abs/2103.15337), full PDF opened and saved `huang-2021.pdf` / `.txt`. Its abstract claims to resolve Heuberger and prove coNP=NP. Two concrete defects prevent using it as a resolution:
sources/literature-notes.md:54:We reject this argument, not assert the negation NP!=coNP. In particular, the unrelated open status of P versus NP would not itself refute an NP=coNP claim.
sources/literature-notes.md:58:Searches used exact question fragments, Heuberger + complexity, NP-hard-forward/P-inverse, Demange–Monnot, weighted Hamming, partial inverse problems, and post-2024 references. The result is a scoped literature check, not an exhaustive proof of absence of any 2026 publication. The strongest positive status finding is the explicit 2009 theorem, verified in the full primary manuscript. The strongest recent broad-open wording is the 2025 publisher abstract. The remaining no-initial-point formulation is marked unresolved **in this dossier**, not falsely certified as a formally established open problem of the entire literature.
proof.md:7:The natural **full-linear, binary, known-feasible-start** version is already solved, not a new open problem. Andreas S. Schulz, *On the Relative Complexity of 15 Problems Related to 0/1-Integer Programming* (2009), DOI [10.1007/978-3-540-76796-1_19](https://doi.org/10.1007/978-3-540-76796-1_19), Assumptions 1–2 and Theorem 1, proves equivalence of optimization, optimality verification, and inverse optimization under the 1- and infinity-norms. Its author manuscript is `sources/schulz-2009.pdf`. The proof below reproduces the essential verification-to-augmentation and bit-scaling arguments (Lemmas 19 and 5), making their promises explicit and extending the zero-test implication to other positive-definite distances. No novelty is claimed for this equivalence.
proof.md:136:Then T belongs to UP, the class of languages recognized by polynomial-time nondeterministic machines with at most one accepting computation per input. Consequently, if T is NP-hard under polynomial many-one reductions, NP=UP. This is a conditional barrier, not a proof that P=NP or that NP!=UP. No literature-novelty claim is made for this elementary consequence.
proof.md:154:Let F be any nonempty family of **nonempty** subsets of E={1,...,n}, with a supplied X0 in F. The forward objective is now genuinely nonlinear:
proof.md:173:Suppose f(c,x)=sum_{j=1}^m c_j phi_j(x), where m is polynomial, every phi_j(x) is binary and polynomial-time computable, all c_j are independently mutable, and phi contains the original coordinates x_1,...,x_n. Then the image F'={phi(x):x in F} is a binary feasible family with polynomial-time membership: read its first n coordinates as x, check x in F, and recompute phi(x). A supplied x0 yields phi(x0), and changing feature costs is exactly the advertised original parameter change. Apply Theorem F to F' and recover x from those first n coordinates. Therefore inverse in P implies forward in P here as well. This covers fully independently parametrized pseudo-Boolean polynomial objectives whose feature lists contain all linear monomials, including full quadratic objectives with linear terms. It does not cover arbitrary nonlinear dependence on costs or tied coefficients.
proof.md:177:The remaining research question is the converse for **general objectives**, not an invented claim that the linear or bottleneck cases are still open. One finite encoded refinement of Heuberger's equations is:
proof.md:181:The attainment/encoding premise is explicit because a general nonlinear inverse constraint need not have a rational or even attained optimum. The normalization f_n(0,x)=0 prevents making the inverse artificially infeasible at inconvenient targets. The supplied start removes feasibility-only hardness. The zero-test lemma still applies, but our linear augmentation proof requires constructing parameter changes that penalize disagreement with a target without otherwise changing comparisons; an arbitrary f_n need not permit those changes. Bit scaling also need not preserve the objective structure. The bottleneck threshold argument relies on a special subset-existence predicate unavailable for arbitrary f_n. Feature lifting works only when the independent-coordinate and recoverability hypotheses of Section 8d hold.
proof.md:183:We have not proved or refuted this general encoded implication. Heuberger's general equations and the 2025 broad-open wording motivate it, but **no opened source states this exact strengthened encoding/normalization refinement verbatim**. Thus its designation here is OPEN as an unresolved research target, not a claim that its precise formulation has a certified community-wide open-status theorem. The book's full general question remains unsolved in this dossier; neither Proposition C nor the rediscovery of Schulz is substituted for it.
proof.md:185:## 9. What is and is not settled
proof.md:187:1. Full-linear binary problems with arbitrary signed costs and a known/easily found feasible start: **settled affirmatively in prior literature (Schulz 2009)**. The preceding proof is a reproduced argument with executable exact checks. Hamming inverse-P implies forward-P follows by the same zero-test implication; no equivalence for sum-Hamming is asserted.
proof.md:189:3. Full-cost binary families with arbitrary implicit feasibility, no supplied/easily found initial point, and no dummy closure: **not resolved here**. Our algorithms need their first feasible target. The restricted-cost examples do not provide a counterexample to this exact remaining statement. Proposition U shows that an NP-hard threshold counterexample would imply NP=UP; Proposition O rules out a completely generic membership/inverse-oracle shortcut, but is not an explicit-input counterexample. We make no claim that the literature has proved this most general remaining formulation open; our search did not settle it.
proof.md:190:4. The known-start minmax/bottleneck converse and independently parametrized recoverable binary-feature objectives also hold, by Sections 8c–8d. The normalized general-objective refinement in Section 8e remains **OPEN here**. Changing constraints/edge lengths, arbitrary real feasible sets, partial target observations, and KKT points in place of global optima require separate theorems. The July 2025 Springer chapter still calls its broad NP-hard-forward/P-inverse question unresolved in its public abstract. This is evidence about that publication, not a contradiction to Schulz's explicitly conditional theorem or proof that every specialized refinement is open.

